### List.of() – truly immutable (Java 9+)

```java
List<String> accounts = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "LOAN5001");
// accounts.add("NEW");  // throws UnsupportedOperationException
// accounts.set(0, "X"); // throws UnsupportedOperationException
// accounts contains null → throws NullPointerException at creation
```

### List.copyOf() – immutable copy (Java 10+)

```java
List<String> mutable = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001"));
List<String> immutable = List.copyOf(mutable);

mutable.add("LOAN5001");  // modifies the original
System.out.println(immutable);  // [SAV2001, CUR3001] – unaffected!
```

### Collections.unmodifiableList() – view only

```java
List<String> original = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001"));
List<String> view = Collections.unmodifiableList(original);

// view.add("NEW");  // throws UnsupportedOperationException
original.add("CUR3001");  // modifies the original
System.out.println(view);  // [SAV2001, CUR3001] – the view changed!
```

This is why `Collections.unmodifiableList` is not truly immutable – it is a read-only wrapper over a mutable list.

### Comparison

| Method | Truly immutable? | Null elements? | Original can modify? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `List.of(...)` | Yes | No (NPE) | N/A (no original) |
| `List.copyOf(list)` | Yes | No (NPE) | No (independent copy) |
| `Collections.unmodifiableList(list)` | No (view) | Yes | Yes (view changes) |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Map.of(), Set.of()** follow the same pattern as List.of().
- **Guava ImmutableList.Builder** provides a builder for constructing immutable lists incrementally.
- **Stream.toList() (Java 16+)** returns an unmodifiable list – similar to `List.copyOf(stream.collect(toList()))`.

### What are common mistakes?

A common mistake is not understanding the concept fully before using it in production banking applications.

**Wrong approach:**
```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TransferService {
    public void transfer(String fromAccount, String toAccount, double amount) {
        // WRONG: using double for money
        double balance = getBalance(fromAccount);
        balance = balance - amount;
    }
    private double getBalance(String id) { return 10000.0; }
}
```

**Correct approach:**
```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferService {
    public void transfer(String fromAccount, String toAccount, BigDecimal amount) {
        // CORRECT: using BigDecimal for money
        BigDecimal balance = getBalance(fromAccount);
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);
    }
    private BigDecimal getBalance(String id) { return new BigDecimal("10000.00"); }
}
```

Always use `BigDecimal` for monetary calculations in banking systems to avoid floating-point precision errors.
